  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the diphthong au [ɔu] to the close-mid front rounded vowel [øː]: the rounding of both elements is kept while the tongue position settles at the front. It is one of the changes that separate Old East Norse from Old West Norse. It started in mid-10th-century Denmark, where runic haugʀ 'mound' gave post-runic hø̄gher, and is reflected in Old Swedish and Old Danish; ey (øy) merged with the same /øː/, and ei became ē. Old West Norse and Old Gutnish kept the diphthongs.",
